WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.320 --> 00:00:04.080
These days I hear the terms&nbsp;
Observability, Monitoring, and APM,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:04.080 --> 00:00:08.560
or Application Performance Management&nbsp;
thrown around seemingly interchangeably,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:08.560 --> 00:00:13.200
but these terms actually mean quite different&nbsp;
things. So let's dive in head first and see an&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:13.200 --> 00:00:19.040
example of how exactly these things differ. So to&nbsp;
start I'm going to start with kind of a Java EE&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:19.040 --> 00:00:25.520
application, it's kind of old school, we'll&nbsp;
go back you know maybe a decade. And let's say&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:25.520 --> 00:00:30.400
that we've got some components in this Java EE app&nbsp;
that actually power it. So something important to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:30.400 --> 00:00:35.680
remember here although we might be using a SOA, or&nbsp;
service oriented architecture, this is not exactly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:35.680 --> 00:00:41.440
microservices. So they're not communicating over&nbsp;
Rest APIs. So you have some inherent advantages&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:41.440 --> 00:00:46.080
here, for example you can take advantage of&nbsp;
like the framework the Java EE framework to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:46.640 --> 00:00:51.120
output log files which will probably all come&nbsp;
out into the same directory and the timestamps&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:51.120 --> 00:00:55.680
match up so things are good. In addition, you&nbsp;
could take advantage of something like an APM&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:55.680 --> 00:01:01.120
solution which is kind of like a one size fits&nbsp;
all set and forget so you install it and it'll&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:01.120 --> 00:01:06.080
kind of get rich analytics and data and metrics&nbsp;
about the running services within the application.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:07.280 --> 00:01:12.080
So essentially what we've done is we've made&nbsp;
our system observable so that you know our&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:12.080 --> 00:01:19.360
Ops teams were then able to kind of look into&nbsp;
it and identify problems and figure out you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:19.360 --> 00:01:24.320
know if anything needed to be done. So for the&nbsp;
business objectives back then this was essentially&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:24.320 --> 00:01:30.080
good enough, but this tends to fall apart very&nbsp;
quickly when you start to move to a more cloud&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:30.080 --> 00:01:34.480
native approach where you have multiple run times&nbsp;
and multiple kind of layers to the architecture.

00:01:36.560 --> 00:01:42.800
So let's say we have an example app here. So we'll&nbsp;
say we'll start with node as a front end. Let's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:42.800 --> 00:01:49.600
say we also have a Java backend application. And&nbsp;
then finally let's say we also have a Python app&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:50.640 --> 00:01:54.960
which is doing some data processing. So let's&nbsp;
see how these things work with each other so&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:54.960 --> 00:01:59.440
the front-end app probably talks to the Java app&nbsp;
and also the Python app for some data processing.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:00.320 --> 00:02:05.280
The Java app probably communicates with a database&nbsp;
and then the Python app probably talks to the Java&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:05.280 --> 00:02:12.640
app for kind of crud operations. So this is kind&nbsp;
of my quick sketch, kind of a dummy layout for a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:12.640 --> 00:02:17.760
microservices based application. You can take it a&nbsp;
step further and even say that this is all running&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:18.720 --> 00:02:24.400
within Kubernetes. So we've got these&nbsp;
container-based applications running in a cluster.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:25.280 --> 00:02:28.720
So immediately the first problem I can&nbsp;
see here is that with multiple runtimes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:28.720 --> 00:02:32.240
we now have to think about multiple&nbsp;
different agents or ways to collect data.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:32.240 --> 00:02:36.320
So instead of just one APM tool we might have&nbsp;
to start thinking about pulling in multiple&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:37.120 --> 00:02:40.160
so how would we con consolidate all&nbsp;
that data right so that's a challenge.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:41.120 --> 00:02:45.440
In addition, let's think about things like&nbsp;
logging. So each of these runtimes probably&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:45.440 --> 00:02:49.200
outputting logs in a different place, and you&nbsp;
know, we have to figure out how we consolidate&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:49.200 --> 00:02:53.440
all those. Maybe we use a log streaming service.&nbsp;
Regardless you can see the complexity starts to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:53.440 --> 00:02:58.800
grow. And finally, as you add more services and&nbsp;
microservices components to this architecture,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:58.800 --> 00:03:04.320
say a user comes in where try to actually access&nbsp;
one of these services and they run into an error&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:04.320 --> 00:03:08.960
you need to trace that request through the&nbsp;
multiple services. Well unless you have the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:08.960 --> 00:03:14.000
right architecture infrastructure in place,&nbsp;
you know something like headers on requests,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:14.880 --> 00:03:18.880
maybe a way to handle web sockets, things are&nbsp;
going to start to get messy and you can see how&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:18.880 --> 00:03:25.120
the technical complexity grows quite large. So&nbsp;
here's where Observability comes in and actually&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:25.120 --> 00:03:30.800
differs, and differs itself from kind of standard&nbsp;
APM tools. It thinks about the more holistic cloud&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:30.800 --> 00:03:35.040
cloud-native approach for being able to do&nbsp;
things like logging and monitoring and that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:35.040 --> 00:03:40.960
kind of thing. So I'll say there's three major&nbsp;
steps for any sort of Observability solution.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:40.960 --> 00:03:46.080
We'll start with the first one we'll call&nbsp;
it collect, because we need to collect data.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:46.080 --> 00:03:51.440
Then we'll go to monitor, and we'll talk about&nbsp;
this because this is you know part of monitoring.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:51.440 --> 00:03:58.080
And finally we'll end with analyze, kind of doing&nbsp;
something with the actual data that you have so&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:58.080 --> 00:04:02.320
with the collect step, you know first thing let's&nbsp;
say that we actually made our system observable.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:03.120 --> 00:04:07.680
So the great thing is with Kubernetes you get&nbsp;
some CPU memory data automatically. So let's say&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:07.680 --> 00:04:12.080
we get some of that, we get some logs from the&nbsp;
application all streaming to the same location&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:13.040 --> 00:04:17.920
and let's say we even get some other stuff like&nbsp;
high availability numbers or average latency,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:17.920 --> 00:04:20.080
you know things that we want to&nbsp;
be able to track and monitor.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:21.200 --> 00:04:24.720
So that brings me to my next step.&nbsp;
So once we have this data available&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:26.320 --> 00:04:31.200
we need to be able to actually do something with&nbsp;
it, at least visualizing it maybe if we're not&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:31.200 --> 00:04:36.320
actually even solving problems yet what do&nbsp;
we do with this data. Well maybe we create&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:36.320 --> 00:04:41.920
some dashboards to be able to monitor the&nbsp;
health of our application, and say we create&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:41.920 --> 00:04:47.440
multiple dashboards to be able to track different&nbsp;
services or kind of different business objectives,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:47.440 --> 00:04:52.960
high availability versus latency, that kind of&nbsp;
thing. Now the final thing that I want to talk&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:52.960 --> 00:04:59.040
about here is what do we do next. So say we found&nbsp;
some bug in the application by kind of looking&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:59.040 --> 00:05:04.160
at our monitoring dashboards and we need to dive&nbsp;
in deeper and fix the problem with the node app.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:05.440 --> 00:05:10.160
Well the great thing about that is an&nbsp;
Observability solution should allow you to do&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:10.160 --> 00:05:16.080
just that, it allows you to actually take it even&nbsp;
a step further because these days with Kubernetes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:16.080 --> 00:05:20.160
you're getting a lot of that information from the&nbsp;
Kubernetes layer. So this is something I want to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:20.160 --> 00:05:25.120
quickly pause and talk about. so with APM tools in&nbsp;
the past they were really kind of focused on kind&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:25.120 --> 00:05:30.080
of like resource constraints, CPU usage, memory&nbsp;
usage, that kind of thing. These days that's been&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:30.080 --> 00:05:36.480
offloaded to the Kubernetes layer, so you know&nbsp;
Observability kind of took APM and evolved it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:36.480 --> 00:05:41.440
to the next stage, pulled it a step up and&nbsp;
enables our users to focus on things like&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:42.080 --> 00:05:46.160
SLOs and SLIs, Service Level Objectives&nbsp;
and Service Level Indicators.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:47.600 --> 00:05:51.360
So these will enable you to actually focus&nbsp;
on things that matter to your business.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:51.360 --> 00:05:55.600
So things like making sure that latencies&nbsp;
are low or that application uptime is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:55.600 --> 00:06:01.120
high. So I think that's kind of the crucial three&nbsp;
steps for any sort of observability solution.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:02.240 --> 00:06:06.960
Let's take a step back again. These&nbsp;
things can be hard to set up on your own&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:06.960 --> 00:06:10.960
with open source projects and capabilities&nbsp;
pulling all the different things together,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:12.000 --> 00:06:17.520
so you might be looking at an Enterprise&nbsp;
Observability Solution and so when you're&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:17.520 --> 00:06:22.080
comparing competitors and looking at building&nbsp;
out your enterprise observability capability&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:22.080 --> 00:06:28.480
I would look at kind of three main&nbsp;
things. Now let's start with automation.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:29.440 --> 00:06:33.440
Now every step of the way we need to make sure&nbsp;
that automation is there to make things easier&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:33.440 --> 00:06:39.520
so let's say that our dev team pushes out a new&nbsp;
version of the node app and go from v1 to v2.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:40.640 --> 00:06:46.000
Now let's say they inadvertently introduced a&nbsp;
bug. Instead of making a bulk API call they now&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:46.000 --> 00:06:51.200
make individual API calls to the Python app. So&nbsp;
in our monitoring dashboard our Ops team's like&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:51.200 --> 00:06:56.320
oh guys something's wrong, the DB app is getting a&nbsp;
lot of requests what's going on? Well you need to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:56.320 --> 00:07:02.080
be able to kind of automatically go back and trace&nbsp;
through the requests and identify what happened.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:02.080 --> 00:07:07.360
That actually brings me to my second point as&nbsp;
well, which is context. It's always important,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:07.360 --> 00:07:13.360
I can spell, to have that context. So automation&nbsp;
is important here because when upgrading to the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:13.360 --> 00:07:17.920
new version a node you want to make sure that the&nbsp;
right agent is automatically installed and kind&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:17.920 --> 00:07:22.560
of the instrumentation is in place so your&nbsp;
dev team doesn't quite have to do that, and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:22.560 --> 00:07:26.640
as new services get added you want your monitoring&nbsp;
dashboards to be automatically updated as well.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:27.280 --> 00:07:32.320
And that context is extremely crucial as with&nbsp;
this example we needed to be able to trace that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:32.320 --> 00:07:37.200
request back to the source of the problem. So once&nbsp;
we've traced that request back to the source with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:37.200 --> 00:07:42.320
that context that we have the third step here&nbsp;
and I think probably one of the most important&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:42.320 --> 00:07:48.880
is action. What do we actually do now? And that&nbsp;
brings me to my last step here the analyze phase,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:48.880 --> 00:07:51.600
which remember we talked about was&nbsp;
kind of an evolution of traditional&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:51.600 --> 00:07:56.000
APM tools to kind of the the way that&nbsp;
Observability tools implement that today.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:56.640 --> 00:08:01.440
So when you get to this step you'll probably want&nbsp;
to look at maybe the SLIs within the node app.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:01.440 --> 00:08:05.760
Maybe dive in deeper, right. So maybe you look&nbsp;
in and you identify that you need to look at&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:05.760 --> 00:08:10.960
application trace logs. So you look in the trace&nbsp;
logs and you identify some problems and you figure&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:10.960 --> 00:08:16.640
out what the what the fix is you tell it to your&nbsp;
dev team you know maybe the last step here is fix&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:17.760 --> 00:08:20.880
and then rinse and repeat for any other&nbsp;
issues that might come up in the future.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:22.160 --> 00:08:26.720
So I think Enterprise Observability is extremely&nbsp;
crucial here when we're kind of looking at&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:26.720 --> 00:08:30.480
the bigger picture because it's not&nbsp;
just about having the individual pieces,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:30.480 --> 00:08:34.240
which again like I said might be quite hard&nbsp;
to set up with purely open source approaches,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:34.800 --> 00:08:39.760
but you want to think about automation to make&nbsp;
sure things are kind of set up seamlessly to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:39.760 --> 00:08:44.960
reduce the overhead on your side. make sure you&nbsp;
have context to be able to see how services work&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:44.960 --> 00:08:50.800
with each other maybe even generate things like&nbsp;
dependency graphs to see the broader view because&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:50.800 --> 00:08:54.880
you might not always have a light board like&nbsp;
this to see the architecture so cleanly. And&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:54.880 --> 00:09:00.400
finally being able to take action when you do find&nbsp;
a problem. So making sure that your Observability&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:00.400 --> 00:09:05.600
solution has a way to automatically pull together&nbsp;
data from multiple sources, multiple services,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:06.240 --> 00:09:13.200
and then figure out what's valid and necessary&nbsp;
for you to be able to make that fix happen. So&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:13.200 --> 00:09:17.840
IBM is invested in making sure our clients can&nbsp;
effectively set up Enterprise Observability&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:17.840 --> 00:09:21.680
with the recent acquisition of Instanta.&nbsp;
To learn more about the acquisition,&nbsp;&nbsp;

